{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-49613" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the definite integral ${\\int }_{2}^{3}\\sqrt{{(5x-6)}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae426fe4764d50adefc8157f87c75298efbf526edbf9eacadf1f395f30e38057"} {"question" "q-49613" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =5x-6 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =5 \\\\ \\text{d}u & =5\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u & =\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=5x-6.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 2 & 3 \\\\ u & 4 & 9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\sqrt{{(5x-6)}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{4}^{9}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{9}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{4}^{9}{u}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{5/2}|}_{4}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}({9}^{5/2}-{4}^{5/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{25}(({3}^{2})^{5/2}-({2}^{2})^{5/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{25}({3}^{5}-{2}^{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{25}(243-32) \\\\  & =\\frac{422}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae426fe4764d50adefc8157f87c75298efbf526edbf9eacadf1f395f30e38057"} {"question" "q-49613" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6b71451326a8cceef5c97f4b9fdd64dfcdf0e1dd855fe23a933e88026175665"} {"question" "q-49613" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae426fe4764d50adefc8157f87c75298efbf526edbf9eacadf1f395f30e38057"} {"question" "q-49613" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{211}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{25}"] ["math" "\\frac{422}{25}"] ["math" "\\frac{484}{25}"] ["math" "\\frac{96}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae426fe4764d50adefc8157f87c75298efbf526edbf9eacadf1f395f30e38057"} {"question" "q-49613" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{422}{25}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae426fe4764d50adefc8157f87c75298efbf526edbf9eacadf1f395f30e38057"} {"question" "q-276950" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the inequality $\\operatorname{log}⁡(2-x)+1\\le \\operatorname{log}⁡(x+9).$\n\n{{field-1}}$\\le \\,x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae426fe4764d50adefc8157f87c75298efbf526edbf9eacadf1f395f30e38057"} {"question" "q-276950" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $10.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2-x)+1} & \\le {10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(x+9)} \\\\ {10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2-x)}\\cdot {10}^{1} & \\le {10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(x+9)} \\\\ (2-x)\\cdot 10 & \\le x+9 \\\\ 20-10x & \\le x+9 \\\\ -11x & \\le -11 \\\\ x & \\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-x & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & <2 \\\\ x+9 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\ge 1,$ which can be expressed as $[1,\\infty ).$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x<2$ and $x>-11,$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,2)$ and $(-11,\\infty )$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n[1,\\infty )\\cap (-\\infty ,2)\\cap (-9,\\infty )=[1,2)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in [1,2),$ which can also be written as $1\\le x<2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae426fe4764d50adefc8157f87c75298efbf526edbf9eacadf1f395f30e38057"} {"question" "q-276950" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6b71451326a8cceef5c97f4b9fdd64dfcdf0e1dd855fe23a933e88026175665"} {"question" "q-276950" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae426fe4764d50adefc8157f87c75298efbf526edbf9eacadf1f395f30e38057"} {"question" "q-276950" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae426fe4764d50adefc8157f87c75298efbf526edbf9eacadf1f395f30e38057"} {"question" "q-276950" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae426fe4764d50adefc8157f87c75298efbf526edbf9eacadf1f395f30e38057"} {"question" "q-276950" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "<"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae426fe4764d50adefc8157f87c75298efbf526edbf9eacadf1f395f30e38057"} {"question" "q-276950" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae426fe4764d50adefc8157f87c75298efbf526edbf9eacadf1f395f30e38057"} {"question" "q-276950" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae426fe4764d50adefc8157f87c75298efbf526edbf9eacadf1f395f30e38057"} {"question" "q-71979" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Write the complex number $z=3-\\text{i}\\sqrt{3}$ in exponential form." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae426fe4764d50adefc8157f87c75298efbf526edbf9eacadf1f395f30e38057"} {"question" "q-71979" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To write our complex number in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{(-\\sqrt{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =2\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=2\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get\n\n$$\nz=2\\sqrt{3}\\,{\\text{e}}^{11\\pi \\text{i}/6}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae426fe4764d50adefc8157f87c75298efbf526edbf9eacadf1f395f30e38057"} {"question" "q-71979" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6b71451326a8cceef5c97f4b9fdd64dfcdf0e1dd855fe23a933e88026175665"} {"question" "q-71979" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae426fe4764d50adefc8157f87c75298efbf526edbf9eacadf1f395f30e38057"} {"question" "q-71979" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "z=2\\sqrt{3}\\,{\\text{e}}^{11\\pi \\text{i}/6}"] ["math" "z=2\\sqrt{3}\\,{\\text{e}}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/3}"] ["math" "z=2\\sqrt{3}\\,{\\text{e}}^{7\\pi \\text{i}/4}"] ["math" "z=4\\sqrt{3}\\,{\\text{e}}^{11\\pi \\text{i}/6}"] ["math" "z=4\\sqrt{3}\\,{\\text{e}}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae426fe4764d50adefc8157f87c75298efbf526edbf9eacadf1f395f30e38057"} {"question" "q-71979" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "z=2\\sqrt{3}\\,{\\text{e}}^{11\\pi \\text{i}/6}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae426fe4764d50adefc8157f87c75298efbf526edbf9eacadf1f395f30e38057"} {"question" "q-316656" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The cubic polynomial $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=5,{x}_{2,3}=-1\\pm 2\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $-4.$ Find $f(x).$\n\n$f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}${x}^{3}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}${x}^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-3}}$x\\,+$\n{{field-4}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae426fe4764d50adefc8157f87c75298efbf526edbf9eacadf1f395f30e38057"} {"question" "q-316656" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $f(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=5$ and ${x}_{2,3}=-1\\pm 2\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $a=-4,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-4(x-5)(x-(-1+2\\text{i}))(x-(-1-2\\text{i})) \\\\  & =-4(x-5)(x-(-1+2\\text{i}))(x-(-1-2\\text{i}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4(x-5)(x-(-1+2\\text{i}))(x-(-1-2\\text{i})) & = \\\\ -4(x-5)({x}^{2}-x(-1-2\\text{i})-x(-1+2\\text{i})+(-1+2\\text{i})(-1-2\\text{i})) & = \\\\ -4(x-5)({x}^{2}+2x+5) & = \\\\ -4({x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+5x-5{x}^{2}-10x-25) & = \\\\ -4({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}-5x-25) & = \\\\ -4{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}+20x+100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=-4{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}+20x+100.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae426fe4764d50adefc8157f87c75298efbf526edbf9eacadf1f395f30e38057"} {"question" "q-316656" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6b71451326a8cceef5c97f4b9fdd64dfcdf0e1dd855fe23a933e88026175665"} {"question" "q-316656" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae426fe4764d50adefc8157f87c75298efbf526edbf9eacadf1f395f30e38057"} {"question" "q-316656" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae426fe4764d50adefc8157f87c75298efbf526edbf9eacadf1f395f30e38057"} {"question" "q-316656" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae426fe4764d50adefc8157f87c75298efbf526edbf9eacadf1f395f30e38057"} {"question" "q-316656" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "12"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae426fe4764d50adefc8157f87c75298efbf526edbf9eacadf1f395f30e38057"} {"question" "q-316656" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae426fe4764d50adefc8157f87c75298efbf526edbf9eacadf1f395f30e38057"} {"question" "q-316656" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "20"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae426fe4764d50adefc8157f87c75298efbf526edbf9eacadf1f395f30e38057"} {"question" "q-316656" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae426fe4764d50adefc8157f87c75298efbf526edbf9eacadf1f395f30e38057"} {"question" "q-316656" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "100"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae426fe4764d50adefc8157f87c75298efbf526edbf9eacadf1f395f30e38057"} {"question" "q-123278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $r(t)=⟨\\sqrt{t+1},\\,t-3⟩,$ $|r(6)|=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae426fe4764d50adefc8157f87c75298efbf526edbf9eacadf1f395f30e38057"} {"question" "q-123278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Evaluating the function at $t=6,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(6) & =⟨\\sqrt{6+1},\\,6-3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\sqrt{7},\\,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the magnitude of this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|r(6)| & =\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{7})}^{2}+{(3)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{7+9} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae426fe4764d50adefc8157f87c75298efbf526edbf9eacadf1f395f30e38057"} {"question" "q-123278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6b71451326a8cceef5c97f4b9fdd64dfcdf0e1dd855fe23a933e88026175665"} {"question" "q-123278" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae426fe4764d50adefc8157f87c75298efbf526edbf9eacadf1f395f30e38057"} {"question" "q-123278" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "7"] ["math" "\\sqrt{10}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{7}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae426fe4764d50adefc8157f87c75298efbf526edbf9eacadf1f395f30e38057"} {"question" "q-123278" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae426fe4764d50adefc8157f87c75298efbf526edbf9eacadf1f395f30e38057"} {"question" "q-129219" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)}?$ \n\n- $(-2,2]$\n- $[-2,4]$\n- $(-2,4)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae426fe4764d50adefc8157f87c75298efbf526edbf9eacadf1f395f30e38057"} {"question" "q-129219" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $(-2,2]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[-2,4]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[-2,4].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval $(-2,4)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae426fe4764d50adefc8157f87c75298efbf526edbf9eacadf1f395f30e38057"} {"question" "q-129219" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f6b71451326a8cceef5c97f4b9fdd64dfcdf0e1dd855fe23a933e88026175665"} {"question" "q-129219" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae426fe4764d50adefc8157f87c75298efbf526edbf9eacadf1f395f30e38057"} {"question" "q-129219" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae426fe4764d50adefc8157f87c75298efbf526edbf9eacadf1f395f30e38057"} {"question" "q-129219" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069307/state.json" "file_sha256" "ae426fe4764d50adefc8157f87c75298efbf526edbf9eacadf1f395f30e38057"}] "usage" {} "basis" 2384 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
